Day 59: Run Batch Predictions on a Dataset
Overview & Objective
In production machine learning systems, online inference via API endpoints (like Flask or FastAPI) handles real-time requests, but offline batch processing is critical for scoring large volumes of historical data, nightly transactions, or scheduled data dumps.

For this task, the goal was to implement a standalone batch scoring script (batch_predict.py) for xFusionCorp Industries to process an overnight transaction batch using a pre-trained fraud-detection model, generating discrete integer class predictions.

Project Architecture & Layout
Plaintext
/root/code/serving/
├── model.pkl            # Deterministic RandomForest model trained on features: amount, hour, num_tx_past_day
├── input.csv            # 10-row batch input dataset containing feature columns (no labels)
├── predictions.csv      # Generated output containing original features plus discrete prediction labels
└── batch_predict.py     # Batch scoring implementation script
Implementation Details (batch_predict.py)
The scoring script performs four core operations:

Model Loading: Unserializes the pre-trained RandomForest classification model from model.pkl using joblib.

Data Ingestion: Reads the batch input features from input.csv using pandas.

Inference Execution: Applies model.predict(df) to score each transaction row, ensuring output labels are strictly cast to integers (0 for normal, 1 for fraudulent transactions).

Result Persist: Appends the prediction column to the original dataset and saves the complete dataframe to predictions.csv without retaining the DataFrame index.

Code Implementation
Python
import pandas as pd
import joblib

MODEL_PATH = "/root/code/serving/model.pkl"
INPUT_CSV = "/root/code/serving/input.csv"
OUTPUT_CSV = "/root/code/serving/predictions.csv"

def main():
    # Load the pre-trained model
    model = joblib.load(MODEL_PATH)
    
    # Read the input features dataset
    df = pd.read_csv(INPUT_CSV)
    
    # Generate integer class predictions (0 or 1)
    predictions = model.predict(df)
    
    # Append the prediction column explicitly as integers
    df["prediction"] = predictions.astype(int)
    
    # Save the output to predictions.csv without index
    df.to_csv(OUTPUT_CSV, index=False)
    print(f"Predictions successfully written to {OUTPUT_CSV}")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
